<p><a id=3D"s1-background"></a>
<a id=3D"1-background"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"background"></a></p>
<h2 id=3D"1-background">1 Background<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"htt=
ps://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#1-background" aria-label=3D"A=
nchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-ico=
ns; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h2>

<p>Python is the main dynamic language used at Google. This style guide is =
a list
of <em>dos and don=E2=80=99ts</em> for Python programs.</p>

<p>To help you format code correctly, we=E2=80=99ve created a <a href=3D"ht=
tps://google.github.io/styleguide/google_python_style.vim">settings file fo=
r Vim</a>. For Emacs, the default settings should be fine.</p>

<p>Many teams use the <a href=3D"https://github.com/psf/black">Black</a> or=
 <a href=3D"https://github.com/google/pyink">Pyink</a>
auto-formatter to avoid arguing over formatting.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2-python-language-rules"></a>
<a id=3D"2-python-language-rules"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"python-language-rules"></a></p>
<h2 id=3D"2-python-language-rules">2 Python Language Rules<a class=3D"ancho=
rjs-link " href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2-pytho=
n-language-rules" aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" st=
yle=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h2>

<p><a id=3D"s2.1-lint"></a>
<a id=3D"21-lint"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"lint"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"21-lint">2.1 Lint<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https://goog=
le.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#21-lint" aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-an=
chorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; padding-le=
ft: 0.375em;"></a></h3>

<p>Run <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">pylint</code> o=
ver your code using this <a href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyl=
intrc">pylintrc</a>.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.1.1-definition"></a>
<a id=3D"211-definition"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"lint-definition"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"211-definition">2.1.1 Definition<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=
=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#211-definition" aria-l=
abel=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anc=
horjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">pylint</code>
is a tool for finding bugs and style problems in Python source code. It fin=
ds
problems that are typically caught by a compiler for less dynamic languages=
 like
C and C++. Because of the dynamic nature of Python, some
warnings may be incorrect; however, spurious warnings should be fairly
infrequent.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.1.2-pros"></a>
<a id=3D"212-pros"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"lint-pros"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"212-pros">2.1.2 Pros<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https://g=
oogle.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#212-pros" aria-label=3D"Anchor" dat=
a-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; paddin=
g-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Catches easy-to-miss errors like typos, using-vars-before-assignment, et=
c.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.1.3-cons"></a>
<a id=3D"213-cons"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"lint-cons"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"213-cons">2.1.3 Cons<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https://g=
oogle.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#213-cons" aria-label=3D"Anchor" dat=
a-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; paddin=
g-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">pylint</code>
isn=E2=80=99t perfect. To take advantage of it, sometimes we=E2=80=99ll nee=
d to write around it,
suppress its warnings or fix it.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.1.4-decision"></a>
<a id=3D"214-decision"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"lint-decision"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"214-decision">2.1.4 Decision<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"h=
ttps://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#214-decision" aria-label=3D=
"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-i=
cons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Make sure you run
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">pylint</code>
on your code.</p>

<p>Suppress warnings if they are inappropriate so that other issues are not=
 hidden.
To suppress warnings, you can set a line-level comment:</p>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"k">def</span> <span class=3D"n=
f">do_PUT</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"bp">self</span><sp=
an class=3D"p">):</span>  <span class=3D"c1"># WSGI name, so pylint: disabl=
e=3Dinvalid-name
</span>  <span class=3D"p">...</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">pylint</code>
warnings are each identified by symbolic name (<code class=3D"language-plai=
ntext highlighter-rouge">empty-docstring</code>)
Google-specific warnings start with <code class=3D"language-plaintext highl=
ighter-rouge">g-</code>.</p>

<p>If the reason for the suppression is not clear from the symbolic name, a=
dd an
explanation.</p>

<p>Suppressing in this way has the advantage that we can easily search for
suppressions and revisit them.</p>

<p>You can get a list of
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">pylint</code>
warnings by doing:</p>

<div class=3D"language-shell highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><p=
re class=3D"highlight"><code>pylint <span class=3D"nt">--list-msgs</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>To get more information on a particular message, use:</p>

<div class=3D"language-shell highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><p=
re class=3D"highlight"><code>pylint <span class=3D"nt">--help-msg</span><sp=
an class=3D"o">=3D</span>invalid-name
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Prefer <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">pylint: disa=
ble</code> to the deprecated older form <code class=3D"language-plaintext h=
ighlighter-rouge">pylint: disable-msg</code>.</p>

<p>Unused argument warnings can be suppressed by deleting the variables at =
the
beginning of the function. Always include a comment explaining why you are
deleting it. =E2=80=9CUnused.=E2=80=9D is sufficient. For example:</p>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"k">def</span> <span class=3D"n=
f">viking_cafe_order</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">spam=
</span><span class=3D"p">:</span> <span class=3D"nb">str</span><span class=
=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"n">beans</span><span class=3D"p">:</span> <s=
pan class=3D"nb">str</span><span class=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"n">egg=
s</span><span class=3D"p">:</span> <span class=3D"nb">str</span> <span clas=
s=3D"o">|</span> <span class=3D"bp">None</span> <span class=3D"o">=3D</span=
> <span class=3D"bp">None</span><span class=3D"p">)</span> <span class=3D"o=
">-&gt;</span> <span class=3D"nb">str</span><span class=3D"p">:</span>
    <span class=3D"k">del</span> <span class=3D"n">beans</span><span class=
=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"n">eggs</span>  <span class=3D"c1"># Unused =
by vikings.
</span>    <span class=3D"k">return</span> <span class=3D"n">spam</span> <s=
pan class=3D"o">+</span> <span class=3D"n">spam</span> <span class=3D"o">+<=
/span> <span class=3D"n">spam</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Other common forms of suppressing this warning include using =E2=80=98<c=
ode class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">_</code>=E2=80=99 as the
identifier for the unused argument or prefixing the argument name with
=E2=80=98<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">unused_</code=
>=E2=80=99, or assigning them to =E2=80=98<code class=3D"language-plaintext=
 highlighter-rouge">_</code>=E2=80=99. These forms are allowed but no longe=
r
encouraged. These break callers that pass arguments by name and do not enfo=
rce
that the arguments are actually unused.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.2-imports"></a>
<a id=3D"22-imports"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"imports"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"22-imports">2.2 Imports<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https:=
//google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#22-imports" aria-label=3D"Anchor=
" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; p=
adding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h3>

<p>Use <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">import</code> s=
tatements for packages and modules only, not for individual types,
classes, or functions.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.2.1-definition"></a>
<a id=3D"221-definition"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"imports-definition"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"221-definition">2.2.1 Definition<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=
=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#221-definition" aria-l=
abel=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anc=
horjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Reusability mechanism for sharing code from one module to another.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.2.2-pros"></a>
<a id=3D"222-pros"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"imports-pros"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"222-pros">2.2.2 Pros<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https://g=
oogle.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#222-pros" aria-label=3D"Anchor" dat=
a-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; paddin=
g-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>The namespace management convention is simple. The source of each identi=
fier is
indicated in a consistent way; <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighte=
r-rouge">x.Obj</code> says that object <code class=3D"language-plaintext hi=
ghlighter-rouge">Obj</code> is defined in
module <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">x</code>.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.2.3-cons"></a>
<a id=3D"223-cons"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"imports-cons"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"223-cons">2.2.3 Cons<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https://g=
oogle.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#223-cons" aria-label=3D"Anchor" dat=
a-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; paddin=
g-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Module names can still collide. Some module names are inconveniently lon=
g.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.2.4-decision"></a>
<a id=3D"224-decision"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"imports-decision"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"224-decision">2.2.4 Decision<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"h=
ttps://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#224-decision" aria-label=3D=
"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-i=
cons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<ul>
  <li>Use <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">import x</co=
de> for importing packages and modules.</li>
  <li>Use <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">from x impor=
t y</code> where <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">x</co=
de> is the package prefix and <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter=
-rouge">y</code> is the module
name with no prefix.</li>
  <li>Use <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">from x impor=
t y as z</code> in any of the following circumstances:
    <ul>
      <li>Two modules named <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-r=
ouge">y</code> are to be imported.</li>
      <li><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">y</code> con=
flicts with a top-level name defined in the current module.</li>
      <li><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">y</code> con=
flicts with a common parameter name that is part of the public
API (e.g., <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">features</c=
ode>).</li>
      <li><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">y</code> is =
an inconveniently long name.</li>
      <li><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">y</code> is =
too generic in the context of your code (e.g., <code class=3D"language-plai=
ntext highlighter-rouge">from
storage.file_system import options as fs_options</code>).</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li>Use <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">import y as =
z</code> only when <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">z</=
code> is a standard abbreviation (e.g., <code class=3D"language-plaintext h=
ighlighter-rouge">import
numpy as np</code>).</li>
</ul>

<p>For example the module <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rou=
ge">sound.effects.echo</code> may be imported as follows:</p>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"kn">from</span> <span class=3D=
"nn">sound.effects</span> <span class=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=3D"n=
">echo</span>
<span class=3D"p">...</span>
<span class=3D"n">echo</span><span class=3D"p">.</span><span class=3D"n">Ec=
hoFilter</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"nb">input</span><sp=
an class=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"n">output</span><span class=3D"p">,<=
/span> <span class=3D"n">delay</span><span class=3D"o">=3D</span><span clas=
s=3D"mf">0.7</span><span class=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"n">atten</span=
><span class=3D"o">=3D</span><span class=3D"mi">4</span><span class=3D"p">)=
</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Do not use relative names in imports. Even if the module is in the same =
package,
use the full package name. This helps prevent unintentionally importing a
package twice.</p>

<p><a id=3D"imports-exemptions"></a></p>
<h5 id=3D"2241-exemptions">2.2.4.1 Exemptions<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " hr=
ef=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2241-exemptions" ari=
a-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 =
anchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h5>

<p>Exemptions from this rule:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Symbols from the following modules are used to support static analysi=
s and
type checking:
    <ul>
      <li><a href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#typin=
g-imports"><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">typing</cod=
e> module</a></li>
      <li><a href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#typin=
g-imports"><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">collections=
.abc</code> module</a></li>
      <li><a href=3D"https://github.com/python/typing_extensions/blob/main/=
README.md"><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">typing_exte=
nsions</code> module</a></li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li>Redirects from the
<a href=3D"https://six.readthedocs.io/#module-six.moves">six.moves module</=
a>.</li>
</ul>

<p><a id=3D"s2.3-packages"></a>
<a id=3D"23-packages"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"packages"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"23-packages">2.3 Packages<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"http=
s://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#23-packages" aria-label=3D"Anc=
hor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons=
; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h3>

<p>Import each module using the full pathname location of the module.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.3.1-pros"></a>
<a id=3D"231-pros"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"packages-pros"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"231-pros">2.3.1 Pros<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https://g=
oogle.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#231-pros" aria-label=3D"Anchor" dat=
a-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; paddin=
g-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Avoids conflicts in module names or incorrect imports due to the module =
search
path not being what the author expected. Makes it easier to find modules.</=
p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.3.2-cons"></a>
<a id=3D"232-cons"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"packages-cons"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"232-cons">2.3.2 Cons<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https://g=
oogle.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#232-cons" aria-label=3D"Anchor" dat=
a-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; paddin=
g-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Makes it harder to deploy code because you have to replicate the package
hierarchy. Not really a problem with modern deployment mechanisms.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.3.3-decision"></a>
<a id=3D"233-decision"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"packages-decision"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"233-decision">2.3.3 Decision<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"h=
ttps://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#233-decision" aria-label=3D=
"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-i=
cons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>All new code should import each module by its full package name.</p>

<p>Imports should be as follows:</p>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"n">Yes</span><span class=3D"p"=
>:</span>
  <span class=3D"c1"># Reference absl.flags in code with the complete name =
(verbose).
</span>  <span class=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=3D"nn">absl.flags</sp=
an>
  <span class=3D"kn">from</span> <span class=3D"nn">doctor.who</span> <span=
 class=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=3D"n">jodie</span>

  <span class=3D"n">_FOO</span> <span class=3D"o">=3D</span> <span class=3D=
"n">absl</span><span class=3D"p">.</span><span class=3D"n">flags</span><spa=
n class=3D"p">.</span><span class=3D"n">DEFINE_string</span><span class=3D"=
p">(...)</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"n">Yes</span><span class=3D"p"=
>:</span>
  <span class=3D"c1"># Reference flags in code with just the module name (c=
ommon).
</span>  <span class=3D"kn">from</span> <span class=3D"nn">absl</span> <spa=
n class=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=3D"n">flags</span>
  <span class=3D"kn">from</span> <span class=3D"nn">doctor.who</span> <span=
 class=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=3D"n">jodie</span>

  <span class=3D"n">_FOO</span> <span class=3D"o">=3D</span> <span class=3D=
"n">flags</span><span class=3D"p">.</span><span class=3D"n">DEFINE_string</=
span><span class=3D"p">(...)</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p><em>(assume this file lives in <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlig=
hter-rouge">doctor/who/</code> where <code class=3D"language-plaintext high=
lighter-rouge">jodie.py</code> also exists)</em></p>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"n">No</span><span class=3D"p">=
:</span>
  <span class=3D"c1"># Unclear what module the author wanted and what will =
be imported.  The actual
</span>  <span class=3D"c1"># import behavior depends on external factors c=
ontrolling sys.path.
</span>  <span class=3D"c1"># Which possible jodie module did the author in=
tend to import?
</span>  <span class=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=3D"nn">jodie</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>The directory the main binary is located in should not be assumed to be =
in
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">sys.path</code> despit=
e that happening in some environments. This being the case,
code should assume that <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge=
">import jodie</code> refers to a third-party or top-level
package named <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">jodie</c=
ode>, not a local <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">jodi=
e.py</code>.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.4-exceptions"></a>
<a id=3D"24-exceptions"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"exceptions"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"24-exceptions">2.4 Exceptions<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"=
https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#24-exceptions" aria-label=
=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorj=
s-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h3>

<p>Exceptions are allowed but must be used carefully.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.4.1-definition"></a>
<a id=3D"241-definition"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"exceptions-definition"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"241-definition">2.4.1 Definition<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=
=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#241-definition" aria-l=
abel=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anc=
horjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Exceptions are a means of breaking out of normal control flow to handle =
errors
or other exceptional conditions.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.4.2-pros"></a>
<a id=3D"242-pros"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"exceptions-pros"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"242-pros">2.4.2 Pros<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https://g=
oogle.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#242-pros" aria-label=3D"Anchor" dat=
a-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; paddin=
g-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>The control flow of normal operation code is not cluttered by error-hand=
ling
code. It also allows the control flow to skip multiple frames when a certai=
n
condition occurs, e.g., returning from N nested functions in one step inste=
ad of
having to plumb error codes through.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.4.3-cons"></a>
<a id=3D"243-cons"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"exceptions-cons"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"243-cons">2.4.3 Cons<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https://g=
oogle.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#243-cons" aria-label=3D"Anchor" dat=
a-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; paddin=
g-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>May cause the control flow to be confusing. Easy to miss error cases whe=
n making
library calls.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.4.4-decision"></a>
<a id=3D"244-decision"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"exceptions-decision"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"244-decision">2.4.4 Decision<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"h=
ttps://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#244-decision" aria-label=3D=
"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-i=
cons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Exceptions must follow certain conditions:</p>

<ul>
  <li>
    <p>Make use of built-in exception classes when it makes sense. For exam=
ple,
raise a <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">ValueError</co=
de> to indicate a programming mistake like a violated
precondition, such as may happen when validating function arguments.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>Do not use <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">asse=
rt</code> statements in place of conditionals or validating
preconditions. They must not be critical to the application logic. A litmus
test would be that the <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"=
>assert</code> could be removed without breaking the code.
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">assert</code> conditio=
nals are
<a href=3D"https://docs.python.org/3/reference/simple_stmts.html#the-assert=
-statement">not guaranteed</a>
to be evaluated. For <a href=3D"https://pytest.org/">pytest</a> based tests=
, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">assert</code> is
okay and expected to verify expectations. For
example:</p>

    <div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highligh=
t"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"n">Yes</span><span class=
=3D"p">:</span>
  <span class=3D"k">def</span> <span class=3D"nf">connect_to_next_port</spa=
n><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"bp">self</span><span class=3D"p"=
>,</span> <span class=3D"n">minimum</span><span class=3D"p">:</span> <span =
class=3D"nb">int</span><span class=3D"p">)</span> <span class=3D"o">-&gt;</=
span> <span class=3D"nb">int</span><span class=3D"p">:</span>
    <span class=3D"s">"""Connects to the next available port.

    Args:
      minimum: A port value greater or equal to 1024.

    Returns:
      The new minimum port.

    Raises:
      ConnectionError: If no available port is found.
    """</span>
    <span class=3D"k">if</span> <span class=3D"n">minimum</span> <span clas=
s=3D"o">&lt;</span> <span class=3D"mi">1024</span><span class=3D"p">:</span=
>
      <span class=3D"c1"># Note that this raising of ValueError is not ment=
ioned in the doc
</span>      <span class=3D"c1"># string's "Raises:" section because it is =
not appropriate to
</span>      <span class=3D"c1"># guarantee this specific behavioral reacti=
on to API misuse.
</span>      <span class=3D"k">raise</span> <span class=3D"nb">ValueError</=
span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"sa">f</span><span class=3D"s"=
>'Min. port must be at least 1024, not </span><span class=3D"si">{</span><s=
pan class=3D"n">minimum</span><span class=3D"si">}</span><span class=3D"s">=
.'</span><span class=3D"p">)</span>
    <span class=3D"n">port</span> <span class=3D"o">=3D</span> <span class=
=3D"bp">self</span><span class=3D"p">.</span><span class=3D"n">_find_next_o=
pen_port</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">minimum</span><s=
pan class=3D"p">)</span>
    <span class=3D"k">if</span> <span class=3D"n">port</span> <span class=
=3D"ow">is</span> <span class=3D"bp">None</span><span class=3D"p">:</span>
      <span class=3D"k">raise</span> <span class=3D"nb">ConnectionError</sp=
an><span class=3D"p">(</span>
          <span class=3D"sa">f</span><span class=3D"s">'Could not connect t=
o service on port </span><span class=3D"si">{</span><span class=3D"n">minim=
um</span><span class=3D"si">}</span><span class=3D"s"> or higher.'</span><s=
pan class=3D"p">)</span>
    <span class=3D"c1"># The code does not depend on the result of this ass=
ert.
</span>    <span class=3D"k">assert</span> <span class=3D"n">port</span> <s=
pan class=3D"o">&gt;=3D</span> <span class=3D"n">minimum</span><span class=
=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"p">(</span>
        <span class=3D"sa">f</span><span class=3D"s">'Unexpected port </spa=
n><span class=3D"si">{</span><span class=3D"n">port</span><span class=3D"si=
">}</span><span class=3D"s"> when minimum was </span><span class=3D"si">{</=
span><span class=3D"n">minimum</span><span class=3D"si">}</span><span class=
=3D"s">.'</span><span class=3D"p">)</span>
    <span class=3D"k">return</span> <span class=3D"n">port</span>
</code></pre></div>    </div>

    <div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highligh=
t"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"n">No</span><span class=3D=
"p">:</span>
  <span class=3D"k">def</span> <span class=3D"nf">connect_to_next_port</spa=
n><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"bp">self</span><span class=3D"p"=
>,</span> <span class=3D"n">minimum</span><span class=3D"p">:</span> <span =
class=3D"nb">int</span><span class=3D"p">)</span> <span class=3D"o">-&gt;</=
