next_open_port</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">minimum</s=
pan><span class=3D"p">)</span>
    <span class=3D"k">assert</span> <span class=3D"n">port</span> <span cla=
ss=3D"ow">is</span> <span class=3D"ow">not</span> <span class=3D"bp">None</=
span>
    <span class=3D"c1"># The type checking of the return statement relies o=
n the assert.
</span>    <span class=3D"k">return</span> <span class=3D"n">port</span>
</code></pre></div>    </div>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>Libraries or packages may define their own exceptions. When doing so=
 they
must inherit from an existing exception class. Exception names should end i=
n
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Error</code> and shoul=
d not introduce repetition (<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-r=
ouge">foo.FooError</code>).</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>Never use catch-all <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-ro=
uge">except:</code> statements, or catch <code class=3D"language-plaintext =
highlighter-rouge">Exception</code> or
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">StandardError</code>, =
unless you are</p>

    <ul>
      <li>re-raising the exception, or</li>
      <li>creating an isolation point in the program where exceptions are n=
ot
propagated but are recorded and suppressed instead, such as protecting a
thread from crashing by guarding its outermost block.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Python is very tolerant in this regard and <code class=3D"language-p=
laintext highlighter-rouge">except:</code> will really catch
everything including misspelled names, sys.exit() calls, Ctrl+C interrupts,
unittest failures and all kinds of other exceptions that you simply don=E2=
=80=99t
want to catch.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>Minimize the amount of code in a <code class=3D"language-plaintext h=
ighlighter-rouge">try</code>/<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-=
rouge">except</code> block. The larger the body
of the <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">try</code>, the=
 more likely that an exception will be raised by a line of
code that you didn=E2=80=99t expect to raise an exception. In those cases, =
the
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">try</code>/<code class=
=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">except</code> block hides a real =
error.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>Use the <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">finally=
</code> clause to execute code whether or not an exception is
raised in the <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">try</cod=
e> block. This is often useful for cleanup, i.e., closing a
file.</p>
  </li>
</ul>

<p><a id=3D"s2.5-global-variables"></a>
<a id=3D"25-global-variables"></a>
<a id=3D"s2.5-global-state"></a>
<a id=3D"25-global-state"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"global-variables"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"25-mutable-global-state">2.5 Mutable Global State<a class=3D"anch=
orjs-link " href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#25-mut=
able-global-state" aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" s=
tyle=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h3>

<p>Avoid mutable global state.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.5.1-definition"></a>
<a id=3D"251-definition"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"global-variables-definition"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"251-definition">2.5.1 Definition<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=
=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#251-definition" aria-l=
abel=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anc=
horjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Module-level values or class attributes that can get mutated during prog=
ram
execution.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.5.2-pros"></a>
<a id=3D"252-pros"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"global-variables-pros"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"252-pros">2.5.2 Pros<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https://g=
oogle.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#252-pros" aria-label=3D"Anchor" dat=
a-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; paddin=
g-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Occasionally useful.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.5.3-cons"></a>
<a id=3D"253-cons"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"global-variables-cons"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"253-cons">2.5.3 Cons<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https://g=
oogle.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#253-cons" aria-label=3D"Anchor" dat=
a-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; paddin=
g-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<ul>
  <li>
    <p>Breaks encapsulation: Such design can make it hard to achieve valid
objectives. For example, if global state is used to manage a database
connection, then connecting to two different databases at the same time
(such as for computing differences during a migration) becomes difficult.
Similar problems easily arise with global registries.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>Has the potential to change module behavior during the import, becau=
se
assignments to global variables are done when the module is first imported.=
</p>
  </li>
</ul>

<p><a id=3D"s2.5.4-decision"></a>
<a id=3D"254-decision"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"global-variables-decision"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"254-decision">2.5.4 Decision<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"h=
ttps://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#254-decision" aria-label=3D=
"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-i=
cons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Avoid mutable global state.</p>

<p>In those rare cases where using global state is warranted, mutable globa=
l
entities should be declared at the module level or as a class attribute and=
 made
internal by prepending an <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rou=
ge">_</code> to the name. If necessary, external access to
mutable global state must be done through public functions or class methods=
. See
<a href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#s3.16-naming">N=
aming</a> below. Please explain the design reasons why mutable
global state is being used in a comment or a doc linked to from a comment.<=
/p>

<p>Module-level constants are permitted and encouraged. For example:
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">_MAX_HOLY_HANDGRENADE_=
COUNT =3D 3</code> for an internal use constant or
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">SIR_LANCELOTS_FAVORITE=
_COLOR =3D "blue"</code> for a public API constant. Constants
must be named using all caps with underscores. See <a href=3D"https://googl=
e.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#s3.16-naming">Naming</a>
below.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.6-nested"></a>
<a id=3D"26-nested"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"nested-classes-functions"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"26-nestedlocalinner-classes-and-functions">2.6 Nested/Local/Inner=
 Classes and Functions<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https://google.gi=
thub.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#26-nestedlocalinner-classes-and-functions" =
aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em /=
 1 anchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h3>

<p>Nested local functions or classes are fine when used to close over a loc=
al
variable. Inner classes are fine.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.6.1-definition"></a>
<a id=3D"261-definition"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"nested-classes-functions-definition"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"261-definition">2.6.1 Definition<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=
=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#261-definition" aria-l=
abel=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anc=
horjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>A class can be defined inside of a method, function, or class. A functio=
n can be
defined inside a method or function. Nested functions have read-only access=
 to
variables defined in enclosing scopes.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.6.2-pros"></a>
<a id=3D"262-pros"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"nested-classes-functions-pros"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"262-pros">2.6.2 Pros<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https://g=
oogle.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#262-pros" aria-label=3D"Anchor" dat=
a-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; paddin=
g-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Allows definition of utility classes and functions that are only used in=
side of
a very limited scope. Very
<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abstract_data_type">ADT</a>-y. Com=
monly used for
implementing decorators.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.6.3-cons"></a>
<a id=3D"263-cons"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"nested-classes-functions-cons"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"263-cons">2.6.3 Cons<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https://g=
oogle.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#263-cons" aria-label=3D"Anchor" dat=
a-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; paddin=
g-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Nested functions and classes cannot be directly tested. Nesting can make=
 the
outer function longer and less readable.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.6.4-decision"></a>
<a id=3D"264-decision"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"nested-classes-functions-decision"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"264-decision">2.6.4 Decision<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"h=
ttps://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#264-decision" aria-label=3D=
"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-i=
cons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>They are fine with some caveats. Avoid nested functions or classes excep=
t when
closing over a local value other than <code class=3D"language-plaintext hig=
hlighter-rouge">self</code> or <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighte=
r-rouge">cls</code>. Do not nest a function
just to hide it from users of a module. Instead, prefix its name with an _ =
at
the module level so that it can still be accessed by tests.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.7-comprehensions"></a>
<a id=3D"s2.7-list_comprehensions"></a>
<a id=3D"27-list_comprehensions"></a>
<a id=3D"list_comprehensions"></a>
<a id=3D"list-comprehensions"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"comprehensions"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"27-comprehensions--generator-expressions">2.7 Comprehensions &amp=
; Generator Expressions<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https://google.g=
ithub.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#27-comprehensions--generator-expressions" =
aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em /=
 1 anchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h3>

<p>Okay to use for simple cases.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.7.1-definition"></a>
<a id=3D"271-definition"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"comprehensions-definition"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"271-definition">2.7.1 Definition<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=
=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#271-definition" aria-l=
abel=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anc=
horjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>List, Dict, and Set comprehensions as well as generator expressions prov=
ide a
concise and efficient way to create container types and iterators without
resorting to the use of traditional loops, <code class=3D"language-plaintex=
t highlighter-rouge">map()</code>, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highli=
ghter-rouge">filter()</code>, or <code class=3D"language-plaintext highligh=
ter-rouge">lambda</code>.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.7.2-pros"></a>
<a id=3D"272-pros"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"comprehensions-pros"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"272-pros">2.7.2 Pros<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https://g=
oogle.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#272-pros" aria-label=3D"Anchor" dat=
a-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; paddin=
g-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Simple comprehensions can be clearer and simpler than other dict, list, =
or set
creation techniques. Generator expressions can be very efficient, since the=
y
avoid the creation of a list entirely.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.7.3-cons"></a>
<a id=3D"273-cons"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"comprehensions-cons"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"273-cons">2.7.3 Cons<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https://g=
oogle.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#273-cons" aria-label=3D"Anchor" dat=
a-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; paddin=
g-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Complicated comprehensions or generator expressions can be hard to read.=
</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.7.4-decision"></a>
<a id=3D"274-decision"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"comprehensions-decision"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"274-decision">2.7.4 Decision<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"h=
ttps://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#274-decision" aria-label=3D=
"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-i=
cons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

