<p><a id=3D"s2.8-default-iterators-and-operators"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"default-iterators-operators"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"28-default-iterators-and-operators">2.8 Default Iterators and Ope=
rators<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https://google.github.io/stylegui=
de/pyguide.html#28-default-iterators-and-operators" aria-label=3D"Anchor" d=
ata-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; padd=
ing-left: 0.375em;"></a></h3>

<p>Use default iterators and operators for types that support them, like li=
sts,
dictionaries, and files.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.8.1-definition"></a>
<a id=3D"281-definition"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"default-iterators-operators-definition"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"281-definition">2.8.1 Definition<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=
=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#281-definition" aria-l=
abel=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anc=
horjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Container types, like dictionaries and lists, define default iterators a=
nd
membership test operators (=E2=80=9Cin=E2=80=9D and =E2=80=9Cnot in=E2=80=
=9D).</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.8.2-pros"></a>
<a id=3D"282-pros"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"default-iterators-operators-pros"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"282-pros">2.8.2 Pros<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https://g=
oogle.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#282-pros" aria-label=3D"Anchor" dat=
a-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; paddin=
g-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>The default iterators and operators are simple and efficient. They expre=
ss the
operation directly, without extra method calls. A function that uses defaul=
t
operators is generic. It can be used with any type that supports the operat=
ion.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.8.3-cons"></a>
<a id=3D"283-cons"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"default-iterators-operators-cons"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"283-cons">2.8.3 Cons<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https://g=
oogle.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#283-cons" aria-label=3D"Anchor" dat=
a-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; paddin=
g-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>You can=E2=80=99t tell the type of objects by reading the method names (=
unless the
variable has type annotations). This is also an advantage.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.8.4-decision"></a>
<a id=3D"284-decision"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"default-iterators-operators-decision"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"284-decision">2.8.4 Decision<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"h=
ttps://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#284-decision" aria-label=3D=
"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-i=
cons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Use default iterators and operators for types that support them, like li=
sts,
dictionaries, and files. The built-in types define iterator methods, too. P=
refer
these methods to methods that return lists, except that you should not muta=
te a
container while iterating over it.</p>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"n">Yes</span><span class=3D"p"=
>:</span>  <span class=3D"k">for</span> <span class=3D"n">key</span> <span =
class=3D"ow">in</span> <span class=3D"n">adict</span><span class=3D"p">:</s=
pan> <span class=3D"p">...</span>
      <span class=3D"k">if</span> <span class=3D"n">obj</span> <span class=
=3D"ow">in</span> <span class=3D"n">alist</span><span class=3D"p">:</span> =
<span class=3D"p">...</span>
      <span class=3D"k">for</span> <span class=3D"n">line</span> <span clas=
s=3D"ow">in</span> <span class=3D"n">afile</span><span class=3D"p">:</span>=
 <span class=3D"p">...</span>
      <span class=3D"k">for</span> <span class=3D"n">k</span><span class=3D=
"p">,</span> <span class=3D"n">v</span> <span class=3D"ow">in</span> <span =
class=3D"n">adict</span><span class=3D"p">.</span><span class=3D"n">items</=
span><span class=3D"p">():</span> <span class=3D"p">...</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"n">No</span><span class=3D"p">=
:</span>   <span class=3D"k">for</span> <span class=3D"n">key</span> <span =
