<p><a id=3D"s2.13-properties"></a>
<a id=3D"213-properties"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"properties"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"213-properties">2.13 Properties<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=
=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#213-properties" aria-l=
abel=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anc=
horjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h3>

<p>Properties may be used to control getting or setting attributes that req=
uire
trivial computations or logic. Property implementations must match the gene=
ral
expectations of regular attribute access: that they are cheap, straightforw=
ard,
and unsurprising.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.13.1-definition"></a>
<a id=3D"2131-definition"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"properties-definition"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"2131-definition">2.13.1 Definition<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " hre=
f=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2131-definition" aria=
-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 a=
nchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>A way to wrap method calls for getting and setting an attribute as a sta=
ndard
attribute access.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.13.2-pros"></a>
<a id=3D"2132-pros"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"properties-pros"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"2132-pros">2.13.2 Pros<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https:/=
/google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2132-pros" aria-label=3D"Anchor" =
data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; pad=
ding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<ul>
  <li>Allows for an attribute access and assignment API rather than
<a href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#getters-and-set=
ters">getter and setter</a> method calls.</li>
  <li>Can be used to make an attribute read-only.</li>
  <li>Allows calculations to be lazy.</li>
  <li>Provides a way to maintain the public interface of a class when the
internals evolve independently of class users.</li>
</ul>

<p><a id=3D"s2.13.3-cons"></a>
<a id=3D"2133-cons"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"properties-cons"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"2133-cons">2.13.3 Cons<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https:/=
/google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2133-cons" aria-label=3D"Anchor" =
data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; pad=
ding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<ul>
  <li>Can hide side-effects much like operator overloading.</li>
  <li>Can be confusing for subclasses.</li>
</ul>

<p><a id=3D"s2.13.4-decision"></a>
<a id=3D"2134-decision"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"properties-decision"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"2134-decision">2.13.4 Decision<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D=
"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2134-decision" aria-label=
=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorj=
s-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Properties are allowed, but, like operator overloading, should only be u=
sed when
necessary and match the expectations of typical attribute access; follow th=
e
<a href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#getters-and-set=
ters">getters and setters</a> rules otherwise.</p>

<p>For example, using a property to simply both get and set an internal att=
ribute
isn=E2=80=99t allowed: there is no computation occurring, so the property i=
s unnecessary
(<a href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#getters-and-se=
tters">make the attribute public instead</a>). In comparison,
using a property to control attribute access or to calculate a <em>triviall=
y</em>
derived value is allowed: the logic is simple and unsurprising.</p>

<p>Properties should be created with the <code class=3D"language-plaintext =
highlighter-rouge">@property</code>
<a href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#s2.17-function-=
and-method-decorators">decorator</a>. Manually implementing a
property descriptor is considered a <a href=3D"https://google.github.io/sty=
leguide/pyguide.html#power-features">power feature</a>.</p>

<p>Inheritance with properties can be non-obvious. Do not use properties to
implement computations a subclass may ever want to override and extend.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.14-truefalse-evaluations"></a>
<a id=3D"214-truefalse-evaluations"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"truefalse-evaluations"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"214-truefalse-evaluations">2.14 True/False Evaluations<a class=3D=
"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2=
14-truefalse-evaluations" aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=
=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a>=
</h3>

<p>Use the =E2=80=9Cimplicit=E2=80=9D false if at all possible (with a few =
caveats).</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.14.1-definition"></a>
<a id=3D"2141-definition"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"truefalse-evaluations-definition"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"2141-definition">2.14.1 Definition<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " hre=
f=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2141-definition" aria=
-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 a=
nchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Python evaluates certain values as <code class=3D"language-plaintext hig=
hlighter-rouge">False</code> when in a boolean context. A quick
=E2=80=9Crule of thumb=E2=80=9D is that all =E2=80=9Cempty=E2=80=9D values =
are considered false, so <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-roug=
e">0, None,
[], {}, ''</code> all evaluate as false in a boolean context.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.14.2-pros"></a>
<a id=3D"2142-pros"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"truefalse-evaluations-pros"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"2142-pros">2.14.2 Pros<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https:/=
/google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2142-pros" aria-label=3D"Anchor" =
data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; pad=
ding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Conditions using Python booleans are easier to read and less error-prone=
. In
most cases, they=E2=80=99re also faster.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.14.3-cons"></a>
<a id=3D"2143-cons"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"truefalse-evaluations-cons"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"2143-cons">2.14.3 Cons<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https:/=
/google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2143-cons" aria-label=3D"Anchor" =
data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; pad=
ding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>May look strange to C/C++ developers.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.14.4-decision"></a>
<a id=3D"2144-decision"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"truefalse-evaluations-decision"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"2144-decision">2.14.4 Decision<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D=
"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2144-decision" aria-label=
=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorj=
s-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Use the =E2=80=9Cimplicit=E2=80=9D false if possible, e.g., <code class=
=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">if foo:</code> rather than <code =
class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">if foo !=3D
[]:</code>. There are a few caveats that you should keep in mind though:</p=
>

<ul>
  <li>
    <p>Always use <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">if f=
oo is None:</code> (or <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"=
>is not None</code>) to check for a <code class=3D"language-plaintext highl=
ighter-rouge">None</code> value.
E.g., when testing whether a variable or argument that defaults to <code cl=
ass=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">None</code>
was set to some other value. The other value might be a value that=E2=80=99=
s false
in a boolean context!</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>Never compare a boolean variable to <code class=3D"language-plaintex=
t highlighter-rouge">False</code> using <code class=3D"language-plaintext h=
ighlighter-rouge">=3D=3D</code>. Use <code class=3D"language-plaintext high=
lighter-rouge">if not x:</code>
instead. If you need to distinguish <code class=3D"language-plaintext highl=
ighter-rouge">False</code> from <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlight=
er-rouge">None</code> then chain the
expressions, such as <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">i=
f not x and x is not None:</code>.</p>
