<h3 id=3D"216-lexical-scoping">2.16 Lexical Scoping<a class=3D"anchorjs-lin=
k " href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#216-lexical-sc=
oping" aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font=
: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h3>

<p>Okay to use.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.16.1-definition"></a>
<a id=3D"2161-definition"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"lexical-scoping-definition"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"2161-definition">2.16.1 Definition<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " hre=
f=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2161-definition" aria=
-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 a=
nchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>A nested Python function can refer to variables defined in enclosing fun=
ctions,
but cannot assign to them. Variable bindings are resolved using lexical sco=
ping,
that is, based on the static program text. Any assignment to a name in a bl=
ock
will cause Python to treat all references to that name as a local variable,=
 even
if the use precedes the assignment. If a global declaration occurs, the nam=
e is
treated as a global variable.</p>

<p>An example of the use of this feature is:</p>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"k">def</span> <span class=3D"n=
f">get_adder</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">summand1</sp=
an><span class=3D"p">:</span> <span class=3D"nb">float</span><span class=3D=
"p">)</span> <span class=3D"o">-&gt;</span> <span class=3D"n">Callable</spa=
n><span class=3D"p">[[</span><span class=3D"nb">float</span><span class=3D"=
p">],</span> <span class=3D"nb">float</span><span class=3D"p">]:</span>
    <span class=3D"s">"""Returns a function that adds numbers to a given nu=
mber."""</span>
    <span class=3D"k">def</span> <span class=3D"nf">adder</span><span class=
=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">summand2</span><span class=3D"p">:</span> =
<span class=3D"nb">float</span><span class=3D"p">)</span> <span class=3D"o"=
>-&gt;</span> <span class=3D"nb">float</span><span class=3D"p">:</span>
        <span class=3D"k">return</span> <span class=3D"n">summand1</span> <=
span class=3D"o">+</span> <span class=3D"n">summand2</span>

    <span class=3D"k">return</span> <span class=3D"n">adder</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p><a id=3D"s2.16.2-pros"></a>
<a id=3D"2162-pros"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"lexical-scoping-pros"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"2162-pros">2.16.2 Pros<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https:/=
/google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2162-pros" aria-label=3D"Anchor" =
data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; pad=
ding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Often results in clearer, more elegant code. Especially comforting to
experienced Lisp and Scheme (and Haskell and ML and =E2=80=A6) programmers.=
</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.16.3-cons"></a>
<a id=3D"2163-cons"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"lexical-scoping-cons"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"2163-cons">2.16.3 Cons<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https:/=
/google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2163-cons" aria-label=3D"Anchor" =
data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; pad=
ding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Can lead to confusing bugs, such as this example based on
<a href=3D"https://peps.python.org/pep-0227/">PEP-0227</a>:</p>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"n">i</span> <span class=3D"o">=
=3D</span> <span class=3D"mi">4</span>
<span class=3D"k">def</span> <span class=3D"nf">foo</span><span class=3D"p"=
>(</span><span class=3D"n">x</span><span class=3D"p">:</span> <span class=
=3D"n">Iterable</span><span class=3D"p">[</span><span class=3D"nb">int</spa=
n><span class=3D"p">]):</span>
    <span class=3D"k">def</span> <span class=3D"nf">bar</span><span class=
=3D"p">():</span>
        <span class=3D"k">print</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=
=3D"n">i</span><span class=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"n">end</span><span=
 class=3D"o">=3D</span><span class=3D"s">''</span><span class=3D"p">)</span=
>
    <span class=3D"c1"># ...
</span>    <span class=3D"c1"># A bunch of code here
</span>    <span class=3D"c1"># ...
</span>    <span class=3D"k">for</span> <span class=3D"n">i</span> <span cl=
ass=3D"ow">in</span> <span class=3D"n">x</span><span class=3D"p">:</span>  =
<span class=3D"c1"># Ah, i *is* local to foo, so this is what bar sees
</span>        <span class=3D"k">print</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><spa=
n class=3D"n">i</span><span class=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"n">end</spa=
n><span class=3D"o">=3D</span><span class=3D"s">''</span><span class=3D"p">=
)</span>
    <span class=3D"n">bar</span><span class=3D"p">()</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>So <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">foo([1, 2, 3])</=
code> will print <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">1 2 3=
 3</code>,
not <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">1 2 3 4</code>.</p=
>

<p><a id=3D"s2.16.4-decision"></a>
<a id=3D"2164-decision"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"lexical-scoping-decision"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"2164-decision">2.16.4 Decision<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D=
"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2164-decision" aria-label=
=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorj=
s-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Okay to use.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.17-function-and-method-decorators"></a>
