<p><a id=3D"decorators"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"217-function-and-method-decorators">2.17 Function and Method Deco=
rators<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https://google.github.io/stylegui=
de/pyguide.html#217-function-and-method-decorators" aria-label=3D"Anchor" d=
ata-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; padd=
ing-left: 0.375em;"></a></h3>

<p>Use decorators judiciously when there is a clear advantage. Avoid <code =
class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">staticmethod</code>
and limit use of <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">class=
method</code>.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.17.1-definition"></a>
<a id=3D"2171-definition"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"decorators-definition"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"2171-definition">2.17.1 Definition<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " hre=
f=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2171-definition" aria=
-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 a=
nchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p><a href=3D"https://docs.python.org/3/glossary.html#term-decorator">Decor=
ators for Functions and Methods</a>
(a.k.a =E2=80=9Cthe <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">@<=
/code> notation=E2=80=9D). One common decorator is <code class=3D"language-=
plaintext highlighter-rouge">@property</code>, used for
converting ordinary methods into dynamically computed attributes. However, =
the
decorator syntax allows for user-defined decorators as well. Specifically, =
for
some function <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">my_decor=
ator</code>, this:</p>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"k">class</span> <span class=3D=
"nc">C</span><span class=3D"p">:</span>
    <span class=3D"o">@</span><span class=3D"n">my_decorator</span>
    <span class=3D"k">def</span> <span class=3D"nf">method</span><span clas=
s=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"bp">self</span><span class=3D"p">):</span>
        <span class=3D"c1"># method body ...
</span></code></pre></div></div>

<p>is equivalent to:</p>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"k">class</span> <span class=3D=
"nc">C</span><span class=3D"p">:</span>
    <span class=3D"k">def</span> <span class=3D"nf">method</span><span clas=
s=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"bp">self</span><span class=3D"p">):</span>
        <span class=3D"c1"># method body ...
</span>    <span class=3D"n">method</span> <span class=3D"o">=3D</span> <sp=
an class=3D"n">my_decorator</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"=
n">method</span><span class=3D"p">)</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p><a id=3D"s2.17.2-pros"></a>
<a id=3D"2172-pros"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"decorators-pros"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"2172-pros">2.17.2 Pros<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https:/=
/google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2172-pros" aria-label=3D"Anchor" =
data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; pad=
ding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Elegantly specifies some transformation on a method; the transformation =
might
eliminate some repetitive code, enforce invariants, etc.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.17.3-cons"></a>
<a id=3D"2173-cons"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"decorators-cons"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"2173-cons">2.17.3 Cons<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https:/=
/google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2173-cons" aria-label=3D"Anchor" =
data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; pad=
ding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Decorators can perform arbitrary operations on a function=E2=80=99s argu=
ments or return
values, resulting in surprising implicit behavior. Additionally, decorators
execute at object definition time. For module-level objects (classes, modul=
e
functions, =E2=80=A6) this happens at import time. Failures in decorator co=
de are
pretty much impossible to recover from.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.17.4-decision"></a>
<a id=3D"2174-decision"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"decorators-decision"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"2174-decision">2.17.4 Decision<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D=
"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2174-decision" aria-label=
=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorj=
s-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Use decorators judiciously when there is a clear advantage. Decorators s=
hould
follow the same import and naming guidelines as functions. Decorator pydoc
should clearly state that the function is a decorator. Write unit tests for
decorators.</p>

<p>Avoid external dependencies in the decorator itself (e.g. don=E2=80=99t =
rely on files,
sockets, database connections, etc.), since they might not be available whe=
n the
decorator runs (at import time, perhaps from <code class=3D"language-plaint=
ext highlighter-rouge">pydoc</code> or other tools). A
decorator that is called with valid parameters should (as much as possible)=
 be
guaranteed to succeed in all cases.</p>

<p>Decorators are a special case of =E2=80=9Ctop-level code=E2=80=9D - see =
<a href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#s3.17-main">mai=
n</a> for
more discussion.</p>

<p>Never use <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">staticmet=
hod</code> unless forced to in order to integrate with an API
defined in an existing library. Write a module-level function instead.</p>

<p>Use <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">classmethod</co=
de> only when writing a named constructor, or a class-specific
routine that modifies necessary global state such as a process-wide cache.<=
/p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.18-threading"></a>
<a id=3D"218-threading"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"threading"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"218-threading">2.18 Threading<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"=
https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#218-threading" aria-label=
=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorj=
s-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h3>

<p>Do not rely on the atomicity of built-in types.</p>

<p>While Python=E2=80=99s built-in data types such as dictionaries appear t=
o have atomic
operations, there are corner cases where they aren=E2=80=99t atomic (e.g. i=
f <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">__hash__</code>
or <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">__eq__</code> are i=
mplemented as Python methods) and their atomicity should not be
relied upon. Neither should you rely on atomic variable assignment (since t=
his
in turn depends on dictionaries).</p>

<p>Use the <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">queue</code=
> module=E2=80=99s <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Que=
ue</code> data type as the preferred way to communicate
data between threads. Otherwise, use the <code class=3D"language-plaintext =
highlighter-rouge">threading</code> module and its locking
primitives. Prefer condition variables and <code class=3D"language-plaintex=
t highlighter-rouge">threading.Condition</code> instead of
using lower-level locks.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.19-power-features"></a>
<a id=3D"219-power-features"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"power-features"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"219-power-features">2.19 Power Features<a class=3D"anchorjs-link =
" href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#219-power-featur=
es" aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1=
em / 1 anchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h3>

<p>Avoid these features.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.19.1-definition"></a>
<a id=3D"2191-definition"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"power-features-definition"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"2191-definition">2.19.1 Definition<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " hre=
f=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2191-definition" aria=
-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 a=
nchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Python is an extremely flexible language and gives you many fancy featur=
es such
as custom metaclasses, access to bytecode, on-the-fly compilation, dynamic
inheritance, object reparenting, import hacks, reflection (e.g. some uses o=
f
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">getattr()</code>), mod=
ification of system internals, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighte=
r-rouge">__del__</code> methods implementing
customized cleanup, etc.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.19.2-pros"></a>
<a id=3D"2192-pros"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"power-features-pros"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"2192-pros">2.19.2 Pros<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https:/=
/google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2192-pros" aria-label=3D"Anchor" =
data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; pad=
ding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>These are powerful language features. They can make your code more compa=
ct.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.19.3-cons"></a>
<a id=3D"2193-cons"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"power-features-cons"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"2193-cons">2.19.3 Cons<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https:/=
/google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2193-cons" aria-label=3D"Anchor" =
data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; pad=
ding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>It=E2=80=99s very tempting to use these =E2=80=9Ccool=E2=80=9D features =
when they=E2=80=99re not absolutely
necessary. It=E2=80=99s harder to read, understand, and debug code that=E2=
=80=99s using unusual
features underneath. It doesn=E2=80=99t seem that way at first (to the orig=
inal author),
but when revisiting the code, it tends to be more difficult than code that =
is
longer but is straightforward.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.19.4-decision"></a>
<a id=3D"2194-decision"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"power-features-decision"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"2194-decision">2.19.4 Decision<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D=
"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2194-decision" aria-label=
=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorj=
s-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Avoid these features in your code.</p>

<p>Standard library modules and classes that internally use these features =
are okay
to use (for example, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">a=
bc.ABCMeta</code>, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">dat=
aclasses</code>, and <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">e=
num</code>).</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.20-modern-python"></a>
<a id=3D"220-modern-python"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"modern-python"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"220-modern-python-from-__future__-imports">2.20 Modern Python: fr=
om __future__ imports<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https://google.git=
hub.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#220-modern-python-from-__future__-imports" a=
ria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / =
1 anchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h3>

<p>New language version semantic changes may be gated behind a special futu=
re
import to enable them on a per-file basis within earlier runtimes.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.20.1-definition"></a>
<a id=3D"2201-definition"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"modern-python-definition"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"2201-definition">2.20.1 Definition<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " hre=
f=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2201-definition" aria=
-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 a=
nchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Being able to turn on some of the more modern features via <code class=
=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">from __future__
import</code> statements allows early use of features from expected future =
Python
versions.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.20.2-pros"></a>
<a id=3D"2202-pros"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"modern-python-pros"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"2202-pros">2.20.2 Pros<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https:/=
/google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2202-pros" aria-label=3D"Anchor" =
data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; pad=
ding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>This has proven to make runtime version upgrades smoother as changes can=
 be made
on a per-file basis while declaring compatibility and preventing regression=
s
within those files. Modern code is more maintainable as it is less likely t=
o
accumulate technical debt that will be problematic during future runtime
upgrades.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.20.3-cons"></a>
<a id=3D"2203-cons"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"modern-python-cons"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"2203-cons">2.20.3 Cons<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https:/=
/google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2203-cons" aria-label=3D"Anchor" =
data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; pad=
ding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Such code may not work on very old interpreter versions prior to the
introduction of the needed future statement. The need for this is more comm=
on in
projects supporting an extremely wide variety of environments.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.20.4-decision"></a>
<a id=3D"2204-decision"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"modern-python-decision"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"2204-decision">2.20.4 Decision<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D=
"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2204-decision" aria-label=
=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorj=
s-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<h5 id=3D"from-__future__-imports">from __future__ imports<a class=3D"ancho=
rjs-link " href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#from-__=
future__-imports" aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" st=
yle=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h5>

<p>Use of <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">from __futur=
e__ import</code> statements is encouraged. It allows a given
source file to start using more modern Python syntax features today. Once y=
ou no
longer need to run on a version where the features are hidden behind a
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">__future__</code> impo=
rt, feel free to remove those lines.</p>

<p>In code that may execute on versions as old as 3.5 rather than &gt;=3D 3=
.7, import:</p>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"kn">from</span> <span class=3D=
"nn">__future__</span> <span class=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=3D"n">g=
enerator_stop</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>For more information read the
<a href=3D"https://docs.python.org/3/library/__future__.html">Python future=
 statement definitions</a>
documentation.</p>

<p>Please don=E2=80=99t remove these imports until you are confident the co=
de is only ever
used in a sufficiently modern environment. Even if you do not currently use=
 the
feature a specific future import enables in your code today, keeping it in =
place
in the file prevents later modifications of the code from inadvertently
depending on the older behavior.</p>

<p>Use other <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">from __fu=
ture__</code> import statements as you see fit.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.21-type-annotated-code"></a>
<a id=3D"s2.21-typed-code"></a>
<a id=3D"221-type-annotated-code"></a>
<a id=3D"typed-code"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"typed-code"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"221-type-annotated-code">2.21 Type Annotated Code<a class=3D"anch=
orjs-link " href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#221-ty=
pe-annotated-code" aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" s=
tyle=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h3>

<p>You can annotate Python code with type hints according to
<a href=3D"https://peps.python.org/pep-0484/">PEP-484</a>, and type-check t=
he code at build
time with a type checking tool like <a href=3D"https://github.com/google/py=
type">pytype</a>.</p>

<p>Type annotations can be in the source or in a
<a href=3D"https://peps.python.org/pep-0484/#stub-files">stub pyi file</a>.=
 Whenever
possible, annotations should be in the source. Use pyi files for third-part=
y or
extension modules.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.21.1-definition"></a>
<a id=3D"2211-definition"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"typed-code-definition"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"2211-definition">2.21.1 Definition<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " hre=
f=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2211-definition" aria=
-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 a=
nchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Type annotations (or =E2=80=9Ctype hints=E2=80=9D) are for function or m=
ethod arguments and
return values:</p>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"k">def</span> <span class=3D"n=
f">func</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">a</span><span cla=
ss=3D"p">:</span> <span class=3D"nb">int</span><span class=3D"p">)</span> <=
span class=3D"o">-&gt;</span> <span class=3D"nb">list</span><span class=3D"=
p">[</span><span class=3D"nb">int</span><span class=3D"p">]:</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>You can also declare the type of a variable using similar
<a href=3D"https://peps.python.org/pep-0526/">PEP-526</a> syntax:</p>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"n">a</span><span class=3D"p">:=
</span> <span class=3D"n">SomeType</span> <span class=3D"o">=3D</span> <spa=
n class=3D"n">some_func</span><span class=3D"p">()</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p><a id=3D"s2.21.2-pros"></a>
<a id=3D"2212-pros"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"typed-code-pros"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"2212-pros">2.21.2 Pros<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https:/=
/google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2212-pros" aria-label=3D"Anchor" =
data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; pad=
ding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Type annotations improve the readability and maintainability of your cod=
e. The
type checker will convert many runtime errors to build-time errors, and red=
uce
your ability to use <a href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.=
html#power-features">Power Features</a>.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.21.3-cons"></a>
<a id=3D"2213-cons"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"typed-code-cons"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"2213-cons">2.21.3 Cons<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https:/=
/google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2213-cons" aria-label=3D"Anchor" =
data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; pad=
ding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>You will have to keep the type declarations up to date.
You might see type errors that you think are
valid code. Use of a
<a href=3D"https://github.com/google/pytype">type checker</a>
may reduce your ability to use <a href=3D"https://google.github.io/stylegui=
de/pyguide.html#power-features">Power Features</a>.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s2.21.4-decision"></a>
<a id=3D"2214-decision"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"typed-code-decision"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"2214-decision">2.21.4 Decision<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D=
"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#2214-decision" aria-label=
=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorj=
s-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>You are strongly encouraged to enable Python type analysis when updating=
 code.
When adding or modifying public APIs, include type annotations and enable
checking via pytype in the build system. As static analysis is relatively n=
ew to
Python, we acknowledge that undesired side-effects (such as
wrongly
inferred types) may prevent adoption by some projects. In those situations,
authors are encouraged to add a comment with a TODO or link to a bug descri=
bing
the issue(s) currently preventing type annotation adoption in the BUILD fil=
e or
in the code itself as appropriate.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s3-python-style-rules"></a>
<a id=3D"3-python-style-rules"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"python-style-rules"></a></p>
<h2 id=3D"3-python-style-rules">3 Python Style Rules<a class=3D"anchorjs-li=
nk " href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#3-python-styl=
e-rules" aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"fo=
nt: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h2>

<p><a id=3D"s3.1-semicolons"></a>
<a id=3D"31-semicolons"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"semicolons"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"31-semicolons">3.1 Semicolons<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"=
https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#31-semicolons" aria-label=
=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorj=
s-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h3>

<p>Do not terminate your lines with semicolons, and do not use semicolons t=
o put
two statements on the same line.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s3.2-line-length"></a>
<a id=3D"32-line-length"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"line-length"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"32-line-length">3.2 Line length<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=
=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#32-line-length" aria-l=
abel=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anc=
horjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h3>

<p>Maximum line length is <em>80 characters</em>.</p>

<p>Explicit exceptions to the 80 character limit:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Long import statements.</li>
  <li>URLs, pathnames, or long flags in comments.</li>
  <li>Long string module-level constants not containing whitespace that wou=
ld be
inconvenient to split across lines such as URLs or pathnames.
    <ul>
      <li>Pylint disable comments. (e.g.: <code class=3D"language-plaintext=
 highlighter-rouge"># pylint: disable=3Dinvalid-name</code>)</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
</ul>

<p>Do not use a backslash for
<a href=3D"https://docs.python.org/3/reference/lexical_analysis.html#explic=
it-line-joining">explicit line continuation</a>.</p>

<p>Instead, make use of Python=E2=80=99s
<a href=3D"http://docs.python.org/reference/lexical_analysis.html#implicit-=
line-joining">implicit line joining inside parentheses, brackets and braces=
</a>.
If necessary, you can add an extra pair of parentheses around an expression=
.</p>

<p>Note that this rule doesn=E2=80=99t prohibit backslash-escaped newlines =
within strings
(see <a href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#strings">b=
elow</a>).</p>

