<p><a id=3D"s3.11-files-sockets-closeables"></a>
<a id=3D"s3.11-files-and-sockets"></a>
<a id=3D"311-files-and-sockets"></a>
<a id=3D"files-and-sockets"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"files"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"311-files-sockets-and-similar-stateful-resources">3.11 Files, Soc=
kets, and similar Stateful Resources<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"htt=
ps://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#311-files-sockets-and-similar=
-stateful-resources" aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B"=
 style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h3>

<p>Explicitly close files and sockets when done with them. This rule natura=
lly
extends to closeable resources that internally use sockets, such as databas=
e
connections, and also other resources that need to be closed down in a simi=
lar
fashion. To name only a few examples, this also includes
<a href=3D"https://docs.python.org/3/library/mmap.html">mmap</a> mappings,
<a href=3D"https://docs.h5py.org/en/stable/high/file.html">h5py File object=
s</a>, and
<a href=3D"https://matplotlib.org/2.1.0/api/_as_gen/matplotlib.pyplot.close=
.html">matplotlib.pyplot figure windows</a>.</p>

<p>Leaving files, sockets or other such stateful objects open unnecessarily=
 has
many downsides:</p>

<ul>
  <li>They may consume limited system resources, such as file descriptors. =
Code
that deals with many such objects may exhaust those resources unnecessarily
if they=E2=80=99re not returned to the system promptly after use.</li>
  <li>Holding files open may prevent other actions such as moving or deleti=
ng
them, or unmounting a filesystem.</li>
  <li>Files and sockets that are shared throughout a program may inadverten=
tly be
read from or written to after logically being closed. If they are actually
closed, attempts to read or write from them will raise exceptions, making
the problem known sooner.</li>
</ul>

<p>Furthermore, while files and sockets (and some similarly behaving resour=
ces) are
automatically closed when the object is destructed, coupling the lifetime o=
f the
object to the state of the resource is poor practice:</p>

<ul>
  <li>There are no guarantees as to when the runtime will actually invoke t=
he
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">__del__</code> method.=
 Different Python implementations use different memory
management techniques, such as delayed garbage collection, which may
increase the object=E2=80=99s lifetime arbitrarily and indefinitely.</li>
  <li>Unexpected references to the file, e.g. in globals or exception trace=
backs,
may keep it around longer than intended.</li>
</ul>

<p>Relying on finalizers to do automatic cleanup that has observable side e=
ffects
has been rediscovered over and over again to lead to major problems, across=
 many
decades and multiple languages (see e.g.
<a href=3D"https://wiki.sei.cmu.edu/confluence/display/java/MET12-J.+Do+not=
+use+finalizers">this article</a>
for Java).</p>

<p>The preferred way to manage files and similar resources is using the
<a href=3D"http://docs.python.org/reference/compound_stmts.html#the-with-st=
atement"><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">with</code> s=
tatement</a>:</p>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"k">with</span> <span class=3D"=
nb">open</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"s">"hello.txt"</spa=
n><span class=3D"p">)</span> <span class=3D"k">as</span> <span class=3D"n">=
hello_file</span><span class=3D"p">:</span>
    <span class=3D"k">for</span> <span class=3D"n">line</span> <span class=
=3D"ow">in</span> <span class=3D"n">hello_file</span><span class=3D"p">:</s=
pan>
        <span class=3D"k">print</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=
=3D"n">line</span><span class=3D"p">)</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>For file-like objects that do not support the <code class=3D"language-pl=
aintext highlighter-rouge">with</code> statement, use
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">contextlib.closing()</=
code>:</p>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=
=3D"nn">contextlib</span>

<span class=3D"k">with</span> <span class=3D"n">contextlib</span><span clas=
s=3D"p">.</span><span class=3D"n">closing</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><=
span class=3D"n">urllib</span><span class=3D"p">.</span><span class=3D"n">u=
rlopen</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"s">"http://www.python=
.org/"</span><span class=3D"p">))</span> <span class=3D"k">as</span> <span =
class=3D"n">front_page</span><span class=3D"p">:</span>
    <span class=3D"k">for</span> <span class=3D"n">line</span> <span class=
=3D"ow">in</span> <span class=3D"n">front_page</span><span class=3D"p">:</s=
pan>
        <span class=3D"k">print</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=
=3D"n">line</span><span class=3D"p">)</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>In rare cases where context-based resource management is infeasible, cod=
e
documentation must explain clearly how resource lifetime is managed.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s3.12-todo-comments"></a>
<a id=3D"312-todo-comments"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"todo"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"312-todo-comments">3.12 TODO Comments<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " =
href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#312-todo-comments"=
 aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em =
/ 1 anchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h3>

<p>Use <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">TODO</code> com=
ments for code that is temporary, a short-term solution, or
good-enough but not perfect.</p>

<p>A <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">TODO</code> comme=
nt begins with the word <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge=
">TODO</code> in all caps, a following colon, and
a link to a resource that contains the context, ideally a bug reference. A =
bug
reference is preferable because bugs are tracked and have follow-up comment=
s.
Follow this piece of context with an explanatory string introduced with a h=
yphen
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-</code>.=20
The purpose is to have a consistent <code class=3D"language-plaintext highl=
ighter-rouge">TODO</code> format that can be searched to find
out how to get more details.</p>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"c1"># TODO: crbug.com/192795 -=
 Investigate cpufreq optimizations.
</span></code></pre></div></div>

<p>Old style, formerly recommended, but discouraged for use in new code:</p=
>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"c1"># TODO(crbug.com/192795): =
Investigate cpufreq optimizations.
# TODO(yourusername): Use a "\*" here for concatenation operator.
</span></code></pre></div></div>

<p>Avoid adding TODOs that refer to an individual or team as the context:</=
p>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"c1"># TODO: @yourusername - Fi=
le an issue and use a '*' for repetition.
</span></code></pre></div></div>

<p>If your <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">TODO</code>=
 is of the form =E2=80=9CAt a future date do something=E2=80=9D make sure t=
hat you
either include a very specific date (=E2=80=9CFix by November 2009=E2=80=9D=
) or a very specific
event (=E2=80=9CRemove this code when all clients can handle XML responses.=
=E2=80=9D) that
future code maintainers will comprehend. Issues are ideal for tracking this=
.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s3.13-imports-formatting"></a>
<a id=3D"313-imports-formatting"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"imports-formatting"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"313-imports-formatting">3.13 Imports formatting<a class=3D"anchor=
js-link " href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#313-impo=
rts-formatting" aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" styl=
e=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h3>

<p>Imports should be on separate lines; there are
<a href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#typing-imports"=
>exceptions for <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">typing=
</code> and <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">collection=
s.abc</code> imports</a>.</p>

<p>E.g.:</p>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"n">Yes</span><span class=3D"p"=
>:</span> <span class=3D"kn">from</span> <span class=3D"nn">collections.abc=
</span> <span class=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=3D"n">Mapping</span><s=
pan class=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"n">Sequence</span>
     <span class=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=3D"nn">os</span>
     <span class=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=3D"nn">sys</span>
     <span class=3D"kn">from</span> <span class=3D"nn">typing</span> <span =
class=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=3D"n">Any</span><span class=3D"p">,<=
/span> <span class=3D"n">NewType</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"n">No</span><span class=3D"p">=
:</span>  <span class=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=3D"nn">os</span><spa=
n class=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"n">sys</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Imports are always put at the top of the file, just after any module com=
ments
and docstrings and before module globals and constants. Imports should be
grouped from most generic to least generic:</p>

<ol>
  <li>
    <p>Python future import statements. For example:</p>

    <div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highligh=
t"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"kn">from</span> <span clas=
s=3D"nn">__future__</span> <span class=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=3D"=
n">annotations</span>
</code></pre></div>    </div>

    <p>See <a href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#from=
-future-imports">above</a> for more information about those.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>Python standard library imports. For example:</p>

    <div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highligh=
t"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"kn">import</span> <span cl=
ass=3D"nn">sys</span>
</code></pre></div>    </div>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p><a href=3D"https://pypi.org/">third-party</a> module
or package imports. For example:</p>

    <div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highligh=
t"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"kn">import</span> <span cl=
ass=3D"nn">tensorflow</span> <span class=3D"k">as</span> <span class=3D"n">=
tf</span>
</code></pre></div>    </div>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>Code repository
sub-package imports. For example:</p>

    <div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highligh=
t"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"kn">from</span> <span clas=
s=3D"nn">otherproject.ai</span> <span class=3D"kn">import</span> <span clas=
s=3D"n">mind</span>
</code></pre></div>    </div>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p><strong>Deprecated:</strong> application-specific imports that are p=
art of the same
top-level
sub-package as this file. For example:</p>

    <div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highligh=
t"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"kn">from</span> <span clas=
s=3D"nn">myproject.backend.hgwells</span> <span class=3D"kn">import</span> =
<span class=3D"n">time_machine</span>
</code></pre></div>    </div>

    <p>You may find older Google Python Style code doing this, but it is no=
 longer
required. <strong>New code is encouraged not to bother with this.</strong> =
Simply treat
application-specific sub-package imports the same as other sub-package
imports.</p>
  </li>
</ol>

<p>Within each grouping, imports should be sorted lexicographically, ignori=
ng case,
according to each module=E2=80=99s full package path (the <code class=3D"la=
nguage-plaintext highlighter-rouge">path</code> in <code class=3D"language-=
plaintext highlighter-rouge">from path import
...</code>). Code may optionally place a blank line between import sections=
.</p>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=
=3D"nn">collections</span>
<span class=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=3D"nn">queue</span>
<span class=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=3D"nn">sys</span>

<span class=3D"kn">from</span> <span class=3D"nn">absl</span> <span class=
=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=3D"n">app</span>
<span class=3D"kn">from</span> <span class=3D"nn">absl</span> <span class=
=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=3D"n">flags</span>
<span class=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=3D"nn">bs4</span>
<span class=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=3D"nn">cryptography</span>
<span class=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=3D"nn">tensorflow</span> <span=
 class=3D"k">as</span> <span class=3D"n">tf</span>

<span class=3D"kn">from</span> <span class=3D"nn">book.genres</span> <span =
class=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=3D"n">scifi</span>
<span class=3D"kn">from</span> <span class=3D"nn">myproject.backend</span> =
<span class=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=3D"n">huxley</span>
<span class=3D"kn">from</span> <span class=3D"nn">myproject.backend.hgwells=
</span> <span class=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=3D"n">time_machine</sp=
an>
<span class=3D"kn">from</span> <span class=3D"nn">myproject.backend.state_m=
achine</span> <span class=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=3D"n">main_loop<=
/span>
<span class=3D"kn">from</span> <span class=3D"nn">otherproject.ai</span> <s=
pan class=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=3D"n">body</span>
<span class=3D"kn">from</span> <span class=3D"nn">otherproject.ai</span> <s=
pan class=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=3D"n">mind</span>
<span class=3D"kn">from</span> <span class=3D"nn">otherproject.ai</span> <s=
pan class=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=3D"n">soul</span>

<span class=3D"c1"># Older style code may have these imports down here inst=
ead:
#from myproject.backend.hgwells import time_machine
#from myproject.backend.state_machine import main_loop
</span></code></pre></div></div>

<p><a id=3D"s3.14-statements"></a>
<a id=3D"314-statements"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"statements"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"314-statements">3.14 Statements<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=
=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#314-statements" aria-l=
abel=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anc=
horjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h3>

<p>Generally only one statement per line.</p>

<p>However, you may put the result of a test on the same line as the test o=
nly if
the entire statement fits on one line. In particular, you can never do so w=
ith
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">try</code>/<code class=
=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">except</code> since the <code cla=
ss=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">try</code> and <code class=3D"l=
anguage-plaintext highlighter-rouge">except</code> can=E2=80=99t both fit o=
n the same line, and
you can only do so with an <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-ro=
uge">if</code> if there is no <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter=
-rouge">else</code>.</p>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"n">Yes</span><span class=3D"p"=
>:</span>

  <span class=3D"k">if</span> <span class=3D"n">foo</span><span class=3D"p"=
>:</span> <span class=3D"n">bar</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=
=3D"n">foo</span><span class=3D"p">)</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"n">No</span><span class=3D"p">=
:</span>

  <span class=3D"k">if</span> <span class=3D"n">foo</span><span class=3D"p"=
>:</span> <span class=3D"n">bar</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=
=3D"n">foo</span><span class=3D"p">)</span>
  <span class=3D"k">else</span><span class=3D"p">:</span>   <span class=3D"=
n">baz</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">foo</span><span cl=
ass=3D"p">)</span>

  <span class=3D"k">try</span><span class=3D"p">:</span>               <spa=
n class=3D"n">bar</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">foo</sp=
an><span class=3D"p">)</span>
  <span class=3D"k">except</span> <span class=3D"nb">ValueError</span><span=
 class=3D"p">:</span> <span class=3D"n">baz</span><span class=3D"p">(</span=
><span class=3D"n">foo</span><span class=3D"p">)</span>

  <span class=3D"k">try</span><span class=3D"p">:</span>
      <span class=3D"n">bar</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"=
n">foo</span><span class=3D"p">)</span>
  <span class=3D"k">except</span> <span class=3D"nb">ValueError</span><span=
 class=3D"p">:</span> <span class=3D"n">baz</span><span class=3D"p">(</span=
><span class=3D"n">foo</span><span class=3D"p">)</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p><a id=3D"s3.15-accessors"></a>
<a id=3D"s3.15-access-control"></a>
<a id=3D"315-access-control"></a>
<a id=3D"access-control"></a>
<a id=3D"accessors"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"getters-and-setters"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"315-getters-and-setters">3.15 Getters and Setters<a class=3D"anch=
orjs-link " href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#315-ge=
tters-and-setters" aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" s=
tyle=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h3>

<p>Getter and setter functions (also called accessors and mutators) should =
be used
when they provide a meaningful role or behavior for getting or setting a
variable=E2=80=99s value.</p>

<p>In particular, they should be used when getting or setting the variable =
is
complex or the cost is significant, either currently or in a reasonable fut=
ure.</p>

<p>If, for example, a pair of getters/setters simply read and write an inte=
rnal
attribute, the internal attribute should be made public instead. By compari=
son,
if setting a variable means some state is invalidated or rebuilt, it should=
 be a
setter function. The function invocation hints that a potentially non-trivi=
al
operation is occurring. Alternatively, <a href=3D"https://google.github.io/=
styleguide/pyguide.html#properties">properties</a> may be an
option when simple logic is needed, or refactoring to no longer need getter=
s and
setters.</p>

<p>Getters and setters should follow the <a href=3D"https://google.github.i=
o/styleguide/pyguide.html#s3.16-naming">Naming</a> guidelines, such
as <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">get_foo()</code> an=
d <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">set_foo()</code>.</p=
>

<p>If the past behavior allowed access through a property, do not bind the =
new
getter/setter functions to the property. Any code still attempting to acces=
s the
variable by the old method should break visibly so they are made aware of t=
he
change in complexity.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s3.16-naming"></a>
<a id=3D"316-naming"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"naming"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"316-naming">3.16 Naming<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https:=
//google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#316-naming" aria-label=3D"Anchor=
" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; p=
adding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h3>

<p><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">module_name</code>,=
 <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">package_name</code>, =
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">ClassName</code>, <cod=
e class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">method_name</code>, <code =
class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">ExceptionName</code>,
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">function_name</code>, =
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">GLOBAL_CONSTANT_NAME</=
code>, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">global_var_name=
</code>, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">instance_var_=
name</code>,
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">function_parameter_nam=
e</code>, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">local_var_na=
me</code>, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">query_prope=
r_noun_for_thing</code>,
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">send_acronym_via_https=
</code>.</p>

<p>Function names, variable names, and filenames should be descriptive; avo=
id
abbreviation. In particular, do not use abbreviations that are ambiguous or
unfamiliar to readers outside your project, and do not abbreviate by deleti=
ng
letters within a word.</p>

<p>Always use a <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">.py</c=
ode> filename extension. Never use dashes.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s3.16.1-names-to-avoid"></a>
<a id=3D"3161-names-to-avoid"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"names-to-avoid"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"3161-names-to-avoid">3.16.1 Names to Avoid<a class=3D"anchorjs-li=
nk " href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#3161-names-to=
-avoid" aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"fon=
t: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<ul>
  <li>
    <p>single character names, except for specifically allowed cases:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>counters or iterators (e.g. <code class=3D"language-plaintext hig=
hlighter-rouge">i</code>, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rou=
ge">j</code>, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">k</code>=
, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">v</code>, et al.)</l=
i>
      <li><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">e</code> as =
an exception identifier in <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-ro=
uge">try/except</code> statements.</li>
      <li><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">f</code> as =
a file handle in <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">with<=
/code> statements</li>
      <li>private <a href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.ht=
ml#typing-type-var">type variables</a> with no constraints (e.g.
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">_T =3D TypeVar("_T")</=
code>, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">_P =3D ParamSpe=
c("_P")</code>)</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Please be mindful not to abuse single-character naming. Generally sp=
eaking,
descriptiveness should be proportional to the name=E2=80=99s scope of visib=
ility.
For example, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">i</code> =
might be a fine name for 5-line code block but within
multiple nested scopes, it is likely too vague.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>dashes (<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-</code=
>) in any package/module name</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">__double_leadin=
g_and_trailing_underscore__</code> names (reserved by Python)</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>offensive terms</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>names that needlessly include the type of the variable (for example:
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">id_to_name_dict</code>=
)</p>
  </li>
</ul>

<p><a id=3D"s3.16.2-naming-conventions"></a>
<a id=3D"3162-naming-convention"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"naming-conventions"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"3162-naming-conventions">3.16.2 Naming Conventions<a class=3D"anc=
horjs-link " href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#3162-=
naming-conventions" aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" =
style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<ul>
  <li>
    <p>=E2=80=9CInternal=E2=80=9D means internal to a module, or protected =
or private within a
class.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>Prepending a single underscore (<code class=3D"language-plaintext hi=
ghlighter-rouge">_</code>) has some support for protecting module
variables and functions (linters will flag protected member access). Note
that it is okay for unit tests to access protected constants from the
modules under test.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>Prepending a double underscore (<code class=3D"language-plaintext hi=
ghlighter-rouge">__</code> aka =E2=80=9Cdunder=E2=80=9D) to an instance var=
iable
or method effectively makes the variable or method private to its class
(using name mangling); we discourage its use as it impacts readability and
testability, and isn=E2=80=99t <em>really</em> private. Prefer a single und=
erscore.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>Place related classes and top-level functions together in a
module.
Unlike Java, there is no need to limit yourself to one class per module.</p=
>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>Use CapWords for class names, but lower_with_under.py for module nam=
es.
Although there are some old modules named CapWords.py, this is now
discouraged because it=E2=80=99s confusing when the module happens to be na=
med after
a class. (=E2=80=9Cwait =E2=80=93 did I write <code class=3D"language-plain=
text highlighter-rouge">import StringIO</code> or <code class=3D"language-p=
laintext highlighter-rouge">from StringIO import
StringIO</code>?=E2=80=9D)</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>New <em>unit test</em> files follow PEP 8 compliant lower_with_under=
 method
names, for example, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">te=
st_&lt;method_under_test&gt;_&lt;state&gt;</code>. For consistency(*)
with legacy modules that follow CapWords function names, underscores may
appear in method names starting with <code class=3D"language-plaintext high=
lighter-rouge">test</code> to separate logical components
of the name. One possible pattern is <code class=3D"language-plaintext high=
lighter-rouge">test&lt;MethodUnderTest&gt;_&lt;state&gt;</code>.</p>
  </li>
</ul>

<p><a id=3D"s3.16.3-file-naming"></a>
<a id=3D"3163-file-naming"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"file-naming"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"3163-file-naming">3.16.3 File Naming<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " h=
ref=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#3163-file-naming" a=
ria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / =
1 anchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Python filenames must have a <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlight=
er-rouge">.py</code> extension and must not contain dashes (<code class=3D"=
language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-</code>).
This allows them to be imported and unittested. If you want an executable t=
o be
accessible without the extension, use a symbolic link or a simple bash wrap=
per
containing <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">exec "$0.py=
" "$@"</code>.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s3.16.4-guidelines-derived-from-guidos-recommendations"></a>
<a id=3D"3164-guidelines-derived-from-guidos-recommendations"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"guidelines-derived-from-guidos-recommendations"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"3164-guidelines-derived-from-guidos-recommendations">3.16.4 Guide=
lines derived from <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guido_van_Rossu=
m">Guido</a>=E2=80=99s Recommendations<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"h=
ttps://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#3164-guidelines-derived-fro=
m-guidos-recommendations" aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=
=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a>=
</h4>

<table rules=3D"all" border=3D"1" summary=3D"Guidelines from Guido's Recomm=
endations" cellspacing=3D"2" cellpadding=3D"2">

  <tbody><tr>
    <th>Type</th>
    <th>Public</th>
    <th>Internal</th>
  </tr>

  <tr>
    <td>Packages</td>
    <td><code>lower_with_under</code></td>
    <td></td>
  </tr>

  <tr>
    <td>Modules</td>
    <td><code>lower_with_under</code></td>
    <td><code>_lower_with_under</code></td>
  </tr>

  <tr>
    <td>Classes</td>
    <td><code>CapWords</code></td>
    <td><code>_CapWords</code></td>
  </tr>

  <tr>
    <td>Exceptions</td>
    <td><code>CapWords</code></td>
    <td></td>
  </tr>

  <tr>
    <td>Functions</td>
    <td><code>lower_with_under()</code></td>
    <td><code>_lower_with_under()</code></td>
  </tr>

  <tr>
    <td>Global/Class Constants</td>
    <td><code>CAPS_WITH_UNDER</code></td>
