    <td></td>
  </tr>

</tbody></table>

<p><a id=3D"s3.17-main"></a>
<a id=3D"317-main"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"math-notation"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"3165-mathematical-notation">3.16.5 Mathematical Notation<a class=
=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.htm=
l#3165-mathematical-notation" aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=
=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"><=
/a></h4>

<p>For mathematically heavy code, short variable names that would otherwise=
 violate
the style guide are preferred when they match established notation in a
reference paper or algorithm. When doing so, reference the source of all na=
ming
conventions in a comment or docstring or, if the source is not accessible,
clearly document the naming conventions. Prefer PEP8-compliant
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">descriptive_names</cod=
e> for public APIs, which are much more likely to be
encountered out of context.</p>

<p><a id=3D"main"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"317-main">3.17 Main<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " href=3D"https://go=
ogle.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#317-main" aria-label=3D"Anchor" data=
-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; padding=
-left: 0.375em;"></a></h3>

<p>In Python, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">pydoc</c=
ode> as well as unit tests require modules to be importable. If a
file is meant to be used as an executable, its main functionality should be=
 in a
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">main()</code> function=
, and your code should always check <code class=3D"language-plaintext highl=
ighter-rouge">if __name__ =3D=3D '__main__'</code>
before executing your main program, so that it is not executed when the mod=
ule
is imported.</p>

<p>When using <a href=3D"https://github.com/abseil/abseil-py">absl</a>, use=
 <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">app.run</code>:</p>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"kn">from</span> <span class=3D=
"nn">absl</span> <span class=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=3D"n">app</sp=
an>
<span class=3D"p">...</span>

<span class=3D"k">def</span> <span class=3D"nf">main</span><span class=3D"p=
">(</span><span class=3D"n">argv</span><span class=3D"p">:</span> <span cla=
ss=3D"n">Sequence</span><span class=3D"p">[</span><span class=3D"nb">str</s=
pan><span class=3D"p">]):</span>
    <span class=3D"c1"># process non-flag arguments
</span>    <span class=3D"p">...</span>

<span class=3D"k">if</span> <span class=3D"n">__name__</span> <span class=
=3D"o">=3D=3D</span> <span class=3D"s">'__main__'</span><span class=3D"p">:=
</span>
    <span class=3D"n">app</span><span class=3D"p">.</span><span class=3D"n"=
>run</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">main</span><span cla=
ss=3D"p">)</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Otherwise, use:</p>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"k">def</span> <span class=3D"n=
f">main</span><span class=3D"p">():</span>
    <span class=3D"p">...</span>

<span class=3D"k">if</span> <span class=3D"n">__name__</span> <span class=
=3D"o">=3D=3D</span> <span class=3D"s">'__main__'</span><span class=3D"p">:=
</span>
    <span class=3D"n">main</span><span class=3D"p">()</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>All code at the top level will be executed when the module is imported. =
Be
careful not to call functions, create objects, or perform other operations =
that
should not be executed when the file is being <code class=3D"language-plain=
text highlighter-rouge">pydoc</code>ed.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s3.18-function-length"></a>
<a id=3D"318-function-length"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"function-length"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"318-function-length">3.18 Function length<a class=3D"anchorjs-lin=
k " href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#318-function-l=
ength" aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font=
: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h3>

<p>Prefer small and focused functions.</p>

<p>We recognize that long functions are sometimes appropriate, so no hard l=
imit is
placed on function length. If a function exceeds about 40 lines, think abou=
t
whether it can be broken up without harming the structure of the program.</=
p>

<p>Even if your long function works perfectly now, someone modifying it in =
a few
months may add new behavior. This could result in bugs that are hard to fin=
d.
Keeping your functions short and simple makes it easier for other people to=
 read
and modify your code.</p>

<p>You could find long and complicated functions when working with
some
code. Do not be intimidated by modifying existing code: if working with suc=
h a
function proves to be difficult, you find that errors are hard to debug, or=
 you
want to use a piece of it in several different contexts, consider breaking =
up
the function into smaller and more manageable pieces.</p>

<p><a id=3D"s3.19-type-annotations"></a>
<a id=3D"319-type-annotations"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"type-annotations"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"319-type-annotations">3.19 Type Annotations<a class=3D"anchorjs-l=
ink " href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#319-type-ann=
otations" aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"f=
ont: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h3>

<p><a id=3D"s3.19.1-general-rules"></a>
<a id=3D"s3.19.1-general"></a>
<a id=3D"3191-general-rules"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"typing-general"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"3191-general-rules">3.19.1 General Rules<a class=3D"anchorjs-link=
 " href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#3191-general-ru=
les" aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: =
1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<ul>
  <li>
    <p>Familiarize yourself with <a href=3D"https://peps.python.org/pep-048=
4/">PEP-484</a>.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>Annotating <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">self=
</code> or <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">cls</code> =
is generally not necessary.
<a href=3D"https://docs.python.org/3/library/typing.html#typing.Self"><code=
 class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Self</code></a> can be
used if it is necessary for proper type information, e.g.</p>

    <div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highligh=
t"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"kn">from</span> <span clas=
s=3D"nn">typing</span> <span class=3D"kn">import</span> <span class=3D"n">S=
elf</span>

<span class=3D"k">class</span> <span class=3D"nc">BaseClass</span><span cla=
ss=3D"p">:</span>
  <span class=3D"o">@</span><span class=3D"nb">classmethod</span>
  <span class=3D"k">def</span> <span class=3D"nf">create</span><span class=
=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">cls</span><span class=3D"p">)</span> <span=
 class=3D"o">-&gt;</span> <span class=3D"n">Self</span><span class=3D"p">:<=
/span>
    <span class=3D"p">...</span>

  <span class=3D"k">def</span> <span class=3D"nf">difference</span><span cl=
ass=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"bp">self</span><span class=3D"p">,</span> =
<span class=3D"n">other</span><span class=3D"p">:</span> <span class=3D"n">=
Self</span><span class=3D"p">)</span> <span class=3D"o">-&gt;</span> <span =
class=3D"nb">float</span><span class=3D"p">:</span>
    <span class=3D"p">...</span>
</code></pre></div>    </div>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>Similarly, don=E2=80=99t feel compelled to annotate the return value=
 of <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">__init__</code>
(where <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">None</code> is =
the only valid option).</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>If any other variable or a returned type should not be expressed, us=
e <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Any</code>.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>You are not required to annotate all the functions in a module.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>At least annotate your public APIs.</li>
      <li>Use judgment to get to a good balance between safety and clarity =
on the
one hand, and flexibility on the other.</li>
      <li>Annotate code that is prone to type-related errors (previous bugs=
 or
complexity).</li>
      <li>Annotate code that is hard to understand.</li>
      <li>Annotate code as it becomes stable from a types perspective. In m=
any
cases, you can annotate all the functions in mature code without losing
too much flexibility.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
</ul>

<p><a id=3D"s3.19.2-line-breaking"></a>
<a id=3D"3192-line-breaking"></a></p>

<p><a id=3D"typing-line-breaking"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"3192-line-breaking">3.19.2 Line Breaking<a class=3D"anchorjs-link=
 " href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#3192-line-break=
ing" aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" style=3D"font: =
1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; padding-left: 0.375em;"></a></h4>

<p>Try to follow the existing <a href=3D"https://google.github.io/styleguid=
e/pyguide.html#indentation">indentation</a> rules.</p>

<p>After annotating, many function signatures will become =E2=80=9Cone para=
meter per line=E2=80=9D.
To ensure the return type is also given its own line, a comma can be placed
after the last parameter.</p>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"k">def</span> <span class=3D"n=
f">my_method</span><span class=3D"p">(</span>
    <span class=3D"bp">self</span><span class=3D"p">,</span>
    <span class=3D"n">first_var</span><span class=3D"p">:</span> <span clas=
s=3D"nb">int</span><span class=3D"p">,</span>
    <span class=3D"n">second_var</span><span class=3D"p">:</span> <span cla=
ss=3D"n">Foo</span><span class=3D"p">,</span>
    <span class=3D"n">third_var</span><span class=3D"p">:</span> <span clas=
s=3D"n">Bar</span> <span class=3D"o">|</span> <span class=3D"bp">None</span=
><span class=3D"p">,</span>
<span class=3D"p">)</span> <span class=3D"o">-&gt;</span> <span class=3D"nb=
">int</span><span class=3D"p">:</span>
  <span class=3D"p">...</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Always prefer breaking between variables, and not, for example, between =
variable
names and type annotations. However, if everything fits on the same line, g=
o for
it.</p>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"k">def</span> <span class=3D"n=
f">my_method</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"bp">self</span>=
<span class=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"n">first_var</span><span class=3D=
"p">:</span> <span class=3D"nb">int</span><span class=3D"p">)</span> <span =
class=3D"o">-&gt;</span> <span class=3D"nb">int</span><span class=3D"p">:</=
span>
  <span class=3D"p">...</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>If the combination of the function name, the last parameter, and the ret=
urn type
is too long, indent by 4 in a new line. When using line breaks, prefer putt=
ing
each parameter and the return type on their own lines and aligning the clos=
ing
parenthesis with the <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">d=
ef</code>:</p>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"n">Yes</span><span class=3D"p"=
>:</span>
<span class=3D"k">def</span> <span class=3D"nf">my_method</span><span class=
=3D"p">(</span>
    <span class=3D"bp">self</span><span class=3D"p">,</span>
    <span class=3D"n">other_arg</span><span class=3D"p">:</span> <span clas=
s=3D"n">MyLongType</span> <span class=3D"o">|</span> <span class=3D"bp">Non=
e</span><span class=3D"p">,</span>
<span class=3D"p">)</span> <span class=3D"o">-&gt;</span> <span class=3D"nb=
">tuple</span><span class=3D"p">[</span><span class=3D"n">MyLongType1</span=
><span class=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"n">MyLongType1</span><span class=
=3D"p">]:</span>
  <span class=3D"p">...</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Optionally, the return type may be put on the same line as the last para=
meter:</p>

<div class=3D"language-python highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><=
pre class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"n">Okay</span><span class=3D"p=
">:</span>
<span class=3D"k">def</span> <span class=3D"nf">my_method</span><span class=
=3D"p">(</span>
    <span class=3D"bp">self</span><span class=3D"p">,</span>
    <span class=3D"n">first_var</span><span class=3D"p">:</span> <span clas=
s=3D"nb">int</span><span class=3D"p">,</span>
    <span class=3D"n">second_var</span><span class=3D"p">:</span> <span cla=
ss=3D"nb">int</span><span class=3D"p">)</span> <span class=3D"o">-&gt;</spa=
n> <span class=3D"nb">dict</span><span class=3D"p">[</span><span class=3D"n=
